To generate a 50 Hz signal on an Arduino Due, you can use the following methods:

**1. Hardware Timer Method:**

- **Implementation:** Configure one of the Arduino Due’s hardware timers for a 50 Hz PWM signal. Here's an example using Timer Counter 0 (TC0):

```cpp
void setup() {
  // Enable the peripheral clock for TC0
  pmc_set_writeprotect(false);
  pmc_enable_periph_clk(ID_TC0);

  // Configure TC0, Channel 0 for waveform mode and set the frequency
  TcChannel * t = &(TC0->TC_CHANNEL)[0];
  t->TC_CMR = TC_CMR_TCCLKS_TIMER_CLOCK1 | // MCK/2 clock
              TC_CMR_WAVE |               // Waveform mode
              TC_CMR_WAVSEL_UP_RC;        // Count up to RC
  t->TC_RC = 42000000 / 2 / 50;           // 50 Hz frequency
  t->TC_RA = t->TC_RC / 2;                // 50% duty cycle
  t->TC_CCR = TC_CCR_CLKEN | TC_CCR_SWTRG; // Enable the clock and trigger

  // Set the pin as output and connect to the timer output
  PIOB->PIO_PDR = PIO_PB25B_TIOA0;        // Disable PIO control
  PIOB->PIO_ABSR |= PIO_PB25B_TIOA0;      // Select peripheral B
}

void loop() {
  // Main loop does nothing, timer handles the output
}
```

**Advantages:** Precise frequency generation, minimal CPU load.
**Limitations:** Requires understanding of timer configuration and register settings.

